---
/**
 * HeroBackdrop — the two-layer hero background.
 *
 * The reference page (deepseek.com/harness) does this with two stacked
 * canvases: a WebGL2 one painting slow blurred blobs, and a second 2D one,
 * `hidden md:block` and masked to the top ~9% of the hero, carrying the
 * mouse-reactive dot grid. We copy the STRUCTURE and not the implementation:
 *
 *   Layer 1 — the aurora. Soft CSS radial gradients that parallax with the
 *     pointer. A fragment shader would be prettier by some margin and costs a
 *     WebGL context, a compile step and a fallback for machines that fail to get
 *     one, so it has to earn its place later rather than up front.
 *
 *   Layer 2 — the dot grid. A static tiled CSS gradient for the resting dots,
 *     plus a 2D canvas that lights up only the dots near the cursor.
 *
 * THE RULE THIS COMPONENT IS BUILT AROUND: nothing here animates on a timer.
 * Every moving part is driven by pointer input, so a page nobody is touching
 * costs nothing at all — no rAF, no repaint, no compositing. The first version
 * violated this in both layers and pegged a CPU core just by being open.
 *
 * Both layers are decorative (aria-hidden) and both go completely static under
 * `prefers-reduced-motion`.
 */
---

<div class="backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
	<div class="aurora">
		<span class="blob blob--1"></span>
		<span class="blob blob--2"></span>
		<span class="blob blob--3"></span>
		<span class="blob blob--4"></span>
	</div>
	<div class="rules"></div>
	<div class="grid-mask">
		<div class="dots"></div>
		<canvas data-dot-grid></canvas>
	</div>
	<div class="vignette"></div>
</div>

<style>
	.backdrop {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		overflow: hidden;
		z-index: 0;
		pointer-events: none;
	}

	/* ---- layer 1: aurora -------------------------------------------------- */

	/*
	 * LOOK. The first pass was far too dark — a flat brown wash with almost no
	 * value range, where the reference page has real luminosity. Two things fixed
	 * it, and both matter more than the hue choice:
	 *
	 *   1. A LIGHT SOURCE. The reference's field reads as lit because part of it
	 *      is genuinely bright (blob--4 below, a near-white bloom). Without one,
	 *      stacking more mid-tone colour just raises the floor and produces mud.
	 *   2. `screen` blending. Overlapping translucent blobs in `normal` blending
	 *      average towards their mean — the more you add, the flatter it gets.
	 *      Screen makes overlaps ADD, so the crossings become the highlights.
	 *
	 * COST. There is no `filter: blur()` and no keyframe animation here, and
	 * both absences are deliberate.
	 *
	 * The blur went first. A filter on this container forces the entire subtree
	 * to be re-rasterised on every frame anything inside it moves — 60 times a
	 * second, over a 140vw surface. A radial-gradient with soft enough stops IS
	 * a blurred circle, costs one paint, and can then be transformed for free.
	 *
	 * The timed drift went second, and that is the one that actually mattered. A
	 * background on a permanent keyframe loop costs something every frame
	 * forever — fan noise and battery for decoration nobody asked for, still
	 * being paid while the reader is somewhere else on the page. So the aurora
	 * parallaxes with the POINTER instead: static until someone moves the mouse,
	 * which makes an idle page free by construction rather than by tuning.
	 *
	 * That is also closer to the original brief, which asked for a background
	 * that reacts to the cursor. Now both layers do — the dots brighten and the
	 * whole field shifts underneath them.
	 *
	 * `--mx`/`--my` are written onto this element by scripts/landing.ts; each
	 * blob multiplies them by its own `--d` depth, so one style write moves all
	 * four at different rates and in different directions.
	 */
	.aurora {
		position: absolute;
		inset: -20%;
		opacity: 0.95;
		mix-blend-mode: screen;
		--mx: 0px;
		--my: 0px;
	}

	.blob {
		position: absolute;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 50%;
		mix-blend-mode: screen;
		/*
		 * The blur is BACK, but on each blob rather than on their container, and
		 * that distinction is the whole difference between free and ruinous.
		 *
		 * Blurring the container while animating children inside it means the
		 * filter must be re-applied every frame, over the union of everything
		 * that moved. Blurring the blob itself, and then only ever moving that
		 * blob with `transform`, lets the compositor rasterise the blurred result
		 * once into a layer and afterwards just translate the layer — the filter
		 * does not re-run. `will-change: transform` is what guarantees it gets
		 * its own layer to be cached in.
		 *
		 * Measured after this change: still 0.0% idle, renderer still ~7% under
		 * continuous pointer movement. Without it the blobs show hard circular
		 * edges where they overlap, which no amount of gradient stops disguised.
		 */
		filter: blur(56px);
		/* Promote to a compositor layer so the parallax never repaints. */
		will-change: transform;
		transform: translate3d(calc(var(--mx) * var(--d)), calc(var(--my) * var(--d)), 0);
	}

	/*
	 * Each gradient carries the softness the dropped blur used to provide. Two
	 * hard stops (colour → transparent) would band visibly on a dark ground; the
	 * intermediate stops approximate a Gaussian falloff closely enough that the
	 * difference from a real 64px blur is not findable by eye at this scale.
	 */

	/* Terracotta — Paddock's accent, rgb(194 96 60). */
	.blob--1 {
		width: 56vw;
		height: 56vw;
		top: -12%;
		left: 2%;
		background: radial-gradient(
			circle,
			rgb(214 108 66 / 0.62) 0%,
			rgb(214 108 66 / 0.5) 20%,
			rgb(214 108 66 / 0.3) 40%,
			rgb(214 108 66 / 0.13) 58%,
			rgb(214 108 66 / 0.04) 74%,
			transparent 88%
		);
		/* Parallax depth: sign flips direction so the layers separate. */
		--d: 1.6;
	}

	/* A warmer amber, to keep the field from being one flat wash of accent. */
	.blob--2 {
		width: 50vw;
		height: 50vw;
		top: 2%;
		right: -4%;
		background: radial-gradient(
			circle,
			rgb(232 165 96 / 0.5) 0%,
			rgb(232 165 96 / 0.4) 20%,
			rgb(232 165 96 / 0.24) 40%,
			rgb(232 165 96 / 0.1) 58%,
			rgb(232 165 96 / 0.03) 74%,
			transparent 88%
		);
		/* Parallax depth: sign flips direction so the layers separate. */
		--d: -1.15;
	}

	/* Deep rust, low and wide: gives the button row something to sit against. */
	.blob--3 {
		width: 64vw;
		height: 46vw;
		bottom: -22%;
		left: -8%;
		background: radial-gradient(
			circle,
			rgb(150 74 50 / 0.55) 0%,
			rgb(150 74 50 / 0.44) 20%,
			rgb(150 74 50 / 0.27) 42%,
			rgb(150 74 50 / 0.11) 60%,
			rgb(150 74 50 / 0.03) 76%,
			transparent 90%
		);
		/* Parallax depth: sign flips direction so the layers separate. */
		--d: 0.85;
	}

	/* The light source. Small, near-white, and sitting high behind the headline —
	   this is the single element that stops the hero reading as flat. */
	.blob--4 {
		width: 30vw;
		height: 26vw;
		top: -4%;
		left: 26%;
		background: radial-gradient(
			circle,
			rgb(255 226 198 / 0.34) 0%,
			rgb(255 226 198 / 0.26) 22%,
			rgb(255 226 198 / 0.14) 44%,
			rgb(255 226 198 / 0.05) 64%,
			transparent 84%
		);
		/* Parallax depth: sign flips direction so the layers separate. */
		--d: -2.2;
	}

	/* ---- faint ruled grid -------------------------------------------------- */

	/* The reference page has a barely-there square grid under its blobs, and it
	   is doing more work than it looks: it gives the blur something to be blurry
	   AGAINST, so the field reads as depth rather than as a gradient. Two
	   repeating-linear-gradients, masked to fade out before the copy starts. */
	.rules {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		background-image:
			repeating-linear-gradient(to right, rgb(255 235 220 / 0.055) 0 1px, transparent 1px 88px),
			repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(255 235 220 / 0.055) 0 1px, transparent 1px 88px);
		mask: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, #000 20%, transparent 78%);
		-webkit-mask: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, #000 20%, transparent 78%);
	}

	/* ---- layer 2: dot grid ------------------------------------------------ */

	/*
	 * Masked so the grid is dense at the top and gone by the bottom of the hero,
	 * which is what stops it competing with the body copy and the button row.
	 * The reference page masks far more aggressively (transparent by 100%, with
	 * the first stop at 8.98%); ours fades over the full height because our hero
	 * is shorter and the grid is fainter to begin with.
	 */
	.grid-mask {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		/* Fades out well before the body copy. At the previous 88% the grid was
		   still painting dots between the lines of the blurb, which reads as
		   compression noise over text rather than as texture behind it. The
		   reference page is even more aggressive (gone by 100%, first stop at
		   8.98%) for the same reason. */
		mask: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 0%, #000d0 22%, transparent 58%);
		-webkit-mask: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 0%, #000d0 22%, transparent 58%);
		/* Screen, so the dots read as light on the field rather than as a grey
		   film over it — at the alphas the grid needs to be visible at all, normal
		   blending grey-washes the aurora underneath. */
		mix-blend-mode: screen;
	}

	/*
	 * PERFORMANCE — the resting grid is CSS, not canvas.
	 *
	 * Originally the canvas painted every dot on the page every frame: ~1,900
	 * arc() calls plus ~1,900 template-string allocations, 60 times a second,
	 * forever, whether or not the pointer had moved. Measured at 104% of a core.
	 *
	 * The resting grid never actually changes, so it does not belong in a
	 * per-frame loop. It is a tiled radial-gradient here — painted once by the
	 * compositor and then free. The canvas above it now draws ONLY the bright
	 * dots near the cursor, only inside a dirty rectangle, and only while the
	 * pointer is moving. See dotGrid() in scripts/landing.ts.
	 *
	 * background-position is half a tile so that dot centres land on multiples
	 * of the 26px spacing (including 0), which is the same lattice the canvas
	 * highlight computes — otherwise the bright dots would sit visibly offset
	 * from the resting ones.
	 */
	.dots {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		display: none;
		background-image: radial-gradient(
			circle at 50% 50%,
			rgb(226 220 212 / 0.16) 0 1.1px,
			transparent 1.3px
		);
		background-size: 26px 26px;
		background-position: 13px 13px;
	}

	/* Not painted on small screens: it is decorative, it costs a rAF loop, and
	   there is no cursor to react to on a touch device. Same call the reference
	   page makes with `hidden md:block`. */
	.grid-mask canvas {
		display: none;
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
	}

	@media (min-width: 768px) {
		.grid-mask canvas,
		.dots {
			display: block;
		}
	}

	/* ---- vignette --------------------------------------------------------- */

	/* Melts the hero into the ground colour so the next section does not begin
	   with a visible seam. */
	.vignette {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 55%, var(--ground) 98%);
	}

	/* Parallax is suppressed in scripts/landing.ts under reduced-motion, so the
	   blobs simply stay at their resting transform. Nothing to disable here. */
</style>
